## 5. Track the mouse on it

> In plain words: `onmousemove` runs your function **every time the mouse moves** over the element. The `event` tells you WHERE the mouse is.

```html
<!-- HTML: pass "event" into the function! -->
<img src="kep.jpg" width="300" onmousemove="egerMozog(event)" onmouseleave="egerKimegy()">
<p id="koord"></p>

<script>
function egerMozog(event) {
  let x = event.offsetX;   // X position INSIDE the image (0 = left edge)
  let y = event.offsetY;   // Y position INSIDE the image (0 = top edge)
  document.getElementById("koord").innerHTML = "X: " + x + "  Y: " + y;
}

function egerKimegy() {
  document.getElementById("koord").innerHTML = "The mouse left the image";
}
</script>
```

### Which X/Y do I use?

| Property                  | Measured from                                 | Use it when                                          |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| `event.offsetX / offsetY` | top-left corner of the ELEMENT                | "where am I on the image/box?" ← **most exam tasks** |
| `event.clientX / clientY` | top-left corner of the browser WINDOW         | "where am I on the screen?"                          |
| `event.pageX / pageY`     | top-left of the whole PAGE (counts scrolling) | long scrolling pages                                 |

### All the events you might need

| Event                          | Runs when...                               |
|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| `onclick`                      | clicked                                    |
| `ondblclick`                   | double-clicked                             |
| `onmousemove`                  | the mouse moves over it (fires A LOT)      |
| `onmouseover` / `onmouseenter` | the mouse arrives on it                    |
| `onmouseout` / `onmouseleave`  | the mouse leaves it                        |
| `onkeyup`                      | a key is released while typing in an input |
| `onkeydown`                    | a key is pressed down                      |
| `onchange`                     | an input/select/checkbox value changed     |
| `oninput`                      | every single change while typing           |
| `onload`                       | (on body) the page finished loading        |

> ⚠️ **offsetX acting weird / jumping to small numbers?** The mouse is over a CHILD element (like text inside the box), so offsetX is measured from that child. Fix: give the child `pointer-events: none;` in CSS, or put the child outside the tracked element.

## 6. The ruler: mouse goes 1 → 100, number at bottom middle, click = guess

> **The idea in plain words:**  
> 1. A box (`position: relative`) with a ruler image inside.  
> 2. The ruler is e.g. 500px wide. The mouse X position on it (`offsetX`) is somewhere between 0 and 499.  
> 3. Turn that into 1–100 with math.  
> 4. Write the number into a label at the bottom middle of the box (`position: absolute`).  
> 5. On click, put the number into the guess input and call the guess function.

### The math (memorize this one line)

```js
let szam = Math.floor(event.offsetX / szelesseg * 100) + 1;
```

| Step                                 | Mouse at far left (offsetX = 0) | Middle (250) | Far right (499) |
|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------|--------------|-----------------|
| `offsetX / 500`                      | 0                               | 0.5          | 0.998           |
| `* 100`                              | 0                               | 50           | 99.8            |
| `Math.floor(...)` (cut off decimals) | 0                               | 50           | 99              |
| `+ 1`                                | **1**                           | **51**       | **100**         |

> 💡 **Even easier** if the ruler is EXACTLY 500px wide: 500 / 100 = 5px per number, so  
> `let szam = Math.floor(event.offsetX / 5) + 1;`

### Ruler-only version

```html
<style>
  .doboz {
    position: relative;        /* frame for the absolute number */
    width: 560px;
    height: 200px;
    border: 2px solid black;
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  .vonalzo {
    display: block;
    width: 500px;              /* IMPORTANT: fixed width */
    height: 60px;
    margin: 20px auto;         /* centered in the box */
    cursor: crosshair;
  }
  #mutato {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 10px;              /* bottom ... */
    left: 50%;                 /* ... middle */
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    font-size: 30px;
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>

<div class="doboz">
  <img src="vonalzo.png" class="vonalzo" onmousemove="vonalzoMozog(event)" onclick="vonalzoKatt()">
  <div id="mutato">-</div>
</div>

<script>
let aktualis = 1;   // the number currently under the mouse (needed by the click too)

function vonalzoMozog(event) {
  let szelesseg = event.target.clientWidth;                     // ruler width (500)
  aktualis = Math.floor(event.offsetX / szelesseg * 100) + 1;   // 1..100
  if (aktualis > 100) aktualis = 100;                           // safety
  if (aktualis < 1) aktualis = 1;
  document.getElementById("mutato").innerHTML = aktualis;
}

function vonalzoKatt() {
  alert("You clicked: " + aktualis);    // in the game: put it in the input + guess (see section 9)
}
</script>
```

> 💡 **No ruler image?** Save any ruler picture from Google as `vonalzo.png` next to your html file. **Or** skip the image and draw it with CSS: use a `<div class="vonalzo"></div>` and add this background:

```css
.vonalzo {
  width: 500px;
  height: 60px;
  border: 1px solid #8a6d1c;
  background:
    repeating-linear-gradient(to right, #333 0 1px, transparent 1px 50px) top / 100% 32px no-repeat,  /* long line every 50px (= every 10 numbers) */
    repeating-linear-gradient(to right, #333 0 1px, transparent 1px 5px)  top / 100% 14px no-repeat,  /* short line every 5px (= every number) */
    #f3d27a;                                                                                          /* yellow wood color */
}
```

