## 8. ⭐ Multiplication table (Szorzótábla)

### Step 1 – What is an HTML table?

```html
<table>
  <tr>              <!-- tr = table ROW -->
    <td>1</td>      <!-- td = table CELL (data) -->
    <td>2</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>2</td>
    <td>4</td>
  </tr>
</table>
```

> A table = **rows** (`tr`), and every row has **cells** (`td`). So we need: a loop for the rows, and INSIDE it a loop for the cells.

### Step 2 – How a for loop reads

```css
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  // this runs 10 times: i = 1, 2, 3, ... 10
}
//   let i = 1   -> start at 1
//   i <= 10     -> keep going while i is at most 10
//   i++         -> add 1 after every round
```

### Step 3 – Loop inside a loop

> **i** = which ROW we are in. **j** = which COLUMN we are in. The cell value = **i \* j**.  
> For every 1 round of i, j runs ALL the way 1 → 10. So: row 1 → cells 1..10, row 2 → cells 1..10, ...

| i (row) | j goes  | cells written |
|---------|---------|---------------|
| 1       | 1, 2, 3 | 1 2 3         |
| 2       | 1, 2, 3 | 2 4 6         |
| 3       | 1, 2, 3 | 3 6 9         |

### VERSION A – document.write (the way your class did it)

**Full working file**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="hu">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Szorzótábla</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: Arial; text-align: center; }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;   /* single lines between cells */
    margin: 20px auto;           /* center the table */
  }
  td {
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border: 1px solid #cccccc;
    text-align: center;
  }
  tr:hover td {                  /* ROW under the mouse lights up */
    background-color: #cfe0ff;
  }
  td:hover {                     /* the CELL under the mouse is orange */
    background-color: #ffcc66 !important;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Szorzótábla</h1>

<script>
let meret = 10;                         // size: 10 x 10

document.write("<table>");              // open table
for (let i = 1; i <= meret; i++) {      // ROWS
  document.write("<tr>");               //   open row
  for (let j = 1; j <= meret; j++) {    //   CELLS in this row
    document.write("<td>" + (i * j) + "</td>");
  }
  document.write("</tr>");              //   close row
}
document.write("</table>");             // close table
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

> ⚠️ `document.write` only works WHILE the page is loading (script inside body). If you call it later (e.g. from a button), it **deletes the whole page**. For a button, use VERSION B.

> ⚠️ **Brackets!** `"<td>" + (i * j) + "</td>"`. Without the ( ) around i \* j it can go wrong with + signs, so always put them.

### VERSION B – button + size input + innerHTML (safer, more flexible)

> Instead of writing straight onto the page, we **build one long text** (string) with the whole table, then put it into a div in one go.

**Full working file**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="hu">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Szorzótábla gombbal</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: Arial; text-align: center; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 20px auto; }
  td { width: 36px; height: 36px; border: 1px solid #ccc; text-align: center; }
  tr:hover td { background-color: #cfe0ff; }
  .fejlec { background-color: #ffe08a; font-weight: bold; }   /* first row + first column */
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Szorzótábla</h1>
Méret: <input type="number" id="meret" value="10" min="1" max="20">
<button onclick="general()">Mehet</button>
<div id="tabla"></div>

<script>
function general() {
  let meret = Number(document.getElementById("meret").value);
  let html = "<table>";                     // start the text

  for (let i = 1; i <= meret; i++) {
    html = html + "<tr>";
    for (let j = 1; j <= meret; j++) {
      if (i == 1 || j == 1) {                // first row OR first column -> special color
        html = html + "<td class='fejlec'>" + (i * j) + "</td>";
      } else {
        html = html + "<td>" + (i * j) + "</td>";
      }
    }
    html = html + "</tr>";
  }

  html = html + "</table>";
  document.getElementById("tabla").innerHTML = html;   // put it on the page in ONE go
}

general();   // draw it once when the page opens
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Extra: highlight the COLUMN too (the trick from your class file)

> CSS can light up a row easily (`tr:hover td`), but there is no "column" tag. Trick: give every cell a class with its column number (`oszlop-1`, `oszlop-2`, ...). Then a CSS rule for EACH column: "if the table has a hovered cell of column 3, color all cells of column 3". A loop writes those rules for you.

```html
<script>
let meret = 15;

// 1) write one pair of CSS rules for EACH column
document.write("<style>");
for (let j = 1; j <= meret; j++) {
  document.write(
    "table:has(.oszlop-" + j + ":hover) .oszlop-" + j + " { background-color: #cfe0ff; }" +       // whole column blue
    "table:has(.oszlop-" + j + ":hover) .oszlop-" + j + ":hover { background-color: #ffcc66; }"    // hovered cell orange
  );
}
document.write("</style>");

// 2) the table, every cell gets its column class
document.write("<table>");
for (let i = 1; i <= meret; i++) {
  document.write("<tr>");
  for (let j = 1; j <= meret; j++) {
    document.write("<td class='oszlop-" + j + "'>" + (i * j) + "</td>");
  }
  document.write("</tr>");
}
document.write("</table>");
</script>
